      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name means “red cape,” from the reddish salt flats and cliffs that line its southern coast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabo_Rojo%2C_Puerto_Rico",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Salt mining began by 1525, making Cabo Rojo an early center of the island’s salt industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabo_Rojo%2C_Puerto_Rico",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first church, San José, was established in 1783, marking the start of organized Catholic worship in the municipality.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabo_Rojo%2C_Puerto_Rico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Punta Ostiones, an archaeological site on the coast, once hosted a large group of Archaic Indians.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabo_Rojo%2C_Puerto_Rico",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Cabo Rojo’s saline deposits host rare microbial carpets, one of the few places worldwide where they appear.",
